Decision: {'answer': 'Based on the attached Wikipedia article about Claude Shannon, I can extract the following information:\n\n**Birth Date:** April 30, 1916\n\n**Death Date:** February 24, 2001\n\n**Three Key Contributions to Information Theory:**\n\n1. **Founding Information Theory:** Shannon published "A Mathematical Theory of Communication" in 1948, which established the fundamental principles of information theory. This work introduced the concept of measuring information in bits and laid the groundwork for modern digital communications.\n\n2. **The Shannon Entropy Formula:** He developed the mathematical formulation of entropy (H = -Σ p(x) log₂ p(x)), which quantifies the uncertainty or information content in a message. This became a cornerstone concept in information theory, cryptography, and statistical mechanics.\n\n3. **The Shannon-Hartley Theorem:** Shannon established the theoretical maximum rate at which information can be transmitted over a communication channel with a given bandwidth and noise level (C = B log₂(1 + S/N)). This theorem defined the fundamental limits of reliable communication and continues to guide telecommunications engineering.\n\nThese contributions collectively established information theory as a distinct scientific discipline and provided the mathematical foundation for virtually all modern digital communication systems, from telephones to the internet.', 'tool_call': None}
